mongo1 answered OK for pay-81723 at once, though it was already cut off. The majority write waited 5 seconds and failed. Within about 15 seconds, mongo2 was primary and mongo1 had stepped down: `isWritablePrimary: false`. After the cut healed, pay-81723, which a client was told was written, is gone from the collection. It survives only in mongo1's rollback file, together with the "failed" pay-81724; `bsondump` prints those BSON files as readable JSON. The folder name is the collection's UUID, its id, and it will differ on your machine. The `local.oplog.rs` folder holds the undone oplog entries. mongo1 is primary again at the end because `env/mongo-rs.yml` gives it priority 2: once it had caught up, it called an election and took the role back. The two majority writes survived everything.

